<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <assemblyIdentity
      type="win32"
      name="MoCoLinux.Setup"
      version="0.2.1.0"
      processorArchitecture="amd64" />
  <description>MoCoLinux setup</description>

  <!--
    Ask for elevation up front.

    Everything this program does needs it: creating the driver service,
    bcdedit, writing under Program Files, and the scheduled task that starts
    the guest at logon. Without a manifest, NT 6 and later start it
    unelevated even for an administrator account -- a normal double-click is
    not an elevated one -- so it would get most of the way in and fail at the
    service. Requesting it here means Windows asks once, before anything has
    been written.

    Deliberately NOT declaring a <compatibility> / <supportedOS> block.
    GetVersionEx reports 6.2 on 8.1 and later unless an executable claims
    support for the newer versions, and setup.c depends on that: it only ever
    asks "is this NT 6 or later" (host_is_nt6), and everything that lies about
    being 6.2 has the three properties it cares about -- UAC, kernel-mode code
    signing, Task Scheduler 2.0. Adding supportedOS entries would make
    GetVersionEx tell the truth, which changes nothing useful and invalidates
    the reasoning written beside that function.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="requireAdministrator" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>
</assembly>
